    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Leigh-on-Sea's Old Town sits on a former primary shipping route to London, with mud flats at low tide extending toward the Thames channel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leigh-on-Sea"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s railway station, opened in 1855 and rebuilt in 1934, still uses the original building as Leigh Sailing Club.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leigh-on-Sea"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the English Civil War, local Parliamentarians known as “Chowbenters” defeated Royalist troops in the Battle of Leigh.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://mark-whitfield.com/2026/02/28/leigh-town-lancashire-timeline-history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Leigh’s name derives from the Saxon “Lee”, meaning a clearing in woodland, first recorded in the 12th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leigh-on-Sea"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Grade II listed Leigh Library, built in 1974, preserves the town’s literary heritage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
